The MiR connects over 5 GHz WiFi to the Teltonika, and the signal it gets is very weak — about −87 dBm, right at the edge of usable. 5 GHz is fast but short-range and penetrates walls/obstacles poorly, so at the robot's distance (and as it moves) the link drops easily. In Pause/Ready it barely shows, but on Play the robot streams a lot of real-time telemetry (~150 msgs/sec: odometry, two lasers, pose, diagnostics) over that marginal link → the WiFi can't keep up, packets drop, and the connection collapses. Moving it to 2.4 GHz would help a lot: that band has much better range and penetration (typically 10-15 dB better — −87 could become ~−72, comfortable), giving a stable link that can carry the data when the robot is active.
